  "narrative": "All estimates below are already signed to the corresponding hypothesis direction, so a positive signed estimate supports the registered association. The newly delivered validation evidence changes the conclusion for two major claims and leaves one borderline claim unresolved.\n\nFirst, H8 is now independently validated. Discovery R8 gave a signed effect of approximately +0.396 (95% interval about +0.325 to +0.467), and validation R39 gives a signed effect of approximately +0.434 with an independent interval about +0.329 to +0.539. Relative to the typical magnitude of dependency_USP7 scores around the comparator mean, this is far above the approximately 10% relative-effect benchmark used for this task. The validation result is also larger in the same direction as discovery, so I accept H8 and close its primary investigation. Scientifically, within this supplied dataset, TP53-loss lines show much weaker USP7 dependency, meaning their USP7 scores are less negative than those of TP53-intact lines. This is an observational association, not a causal claim or a claim of external generalizability, but the independent confirmation plus the earlier adenocarcinoma, high-screen-ROC, high-Cas9, and co-alteration-exclusion robustness results make it one of the better-supported dataset-specific findings.\n\nSecond, H31 is validated in the high-screen-ROC stratum. R40 has a signed effect of approximately +0.812 with an independent interval about +0.773 to +0.851. This confirms that the SMARCA4-loss association with stronger SMARCA2 dependency is not restricted to lower-quality screens or the overall population. I accept H31 and close it as a completed robustness refinement. This indirectly strengthens H5, though H5 itself remains accepted without direct independent validation of the unstratified primary comparison. H5 remains active because, if additional validation resources are available, direct independent validation of the primary SMARCA2 association would be a clean final robustness step. The evidence now supports a dataset-specific claim that SMARCA4-loss lines have markedly stronger SMARCA2 dependency, and this effect is not explained by broader ARID1A loss because H44 showed persistence among ARID1A-intact lines while H43 showed that ARID1A loss among SMARCA4-intact lines is negligible.\n\nThird, H3 remains unresolved after validation. Discovery R3 showed a borderline positive signed association of EGFR amplification with stronger EGFR dependency, with estimate about +0.025 and interval just crossing null. Validation R13 gives a smaller signed estimate of about +0.018 with a wide independent interval from about -0.028 to +0.064. The validation point estimate is below the approximate 10% relative benchmark and does not confirm a clinically meaningful difference. I therefore keep H3 unresolved and deferred rather than accepting it as a meaningful association. This is not a strong null claim because the upper interval could still cover a modest clinically meaningful effect, but the current evidence does not justify acceptance.\n\nThe overall scientific picture is dominated by four supported dataset-specific associations. SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H5 accepted, with H31 now independently validated in high-quality screens). TP53 loss is associated with much weaker USP7 dependency (H8 accepted and now independently validated). MSI-high status is associated with much stronger WRN dependency (H9 accepted, with adenocarcinoma, high-ROC, high-Cas9, and co-alteration-exclusion robustness, though primary independent validation has not yet been completed). KEAP1 loss is associated with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H22 accepted and independently validated), which directly opposes the canonical stronger-dependency hypothesis H6, now rejected/closed. These are not causal claims; the associations could reflect correlated genomic structure, cell-line lineage effects, or other unmeasured features. However, the mutual-exclusion and quality-stratification analyses argue against simple explanations based only on low screen quality, low Cas9 activity, broad co-alteration states, or the minor non-adenocarcinoma stratum.\n\nNegative or non-supported findings include: BRAF V600E association with stronger BRAF dependency (H2 rejected), canonical KEAP1 stronger NFE2L2 dependency (H6 rejected), MYC amplification with stronger DHX9/RPL11/POLR2A dependency (H10-H12 rejected), KRAS mutation with altered RIT1 dependency (H16 rejected), and ARID1A loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency among SMARCA4-intact lines (H43 rejected). These negative results are useful because they narrow interpretation, especially the BAF-pathway specificity: the large SMARCA2 effect tracks with SMARCA4 loss rather than with ARID1A loss in this dataset.\n\nUnresolved or low-confidence signals include the KRAS and EGFR driver-marker primary comparisons, several MYC/RTK-associated weak dependencies, MDM2-TP53, and WRN-BRCA2 extensions. The WRN-BRCA2 results do not currently support a broad DNA-repair extension of MSI-driven WRN dependency, but the intervals are not tight enough for definitive rejection. The most informative next analyses should focus on either completing independent validation for the still-unvalidated large accepted effects, especially H9 and possibly H5, or closing the remaining weak driver-marker hypotheses using high-quality and lineage-specific strata. I have registered one new high-quality adenocarcinoma ERBB2 follow-up to allow a focused closure or upgrade decision for that weak RTK-amplification signal. The next analysis batch should prioritize H62 and H61 for full co-alteration specificity, H48 for KRAS co-driver-excluded dependency_KRAS, H41 and the new ERBB2 refinement for driver amplification dependencies, H40 for TP53-MDM2 under high Cas9, and H42 for WRN under higher mutation burden.",
  "research_notes": "Current supported dataset-specific accepted associations: H5 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H8 TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency, now independently validated; H9 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency, robust but primary independent validation still pending; H22 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, independently validated. H6 rejected canonical KEAP1 stronger NFE2L2. H31 accepted/closed and independently validated in high-screen-ROC stratum. H3 unresolved/deferred because independent validation R13 was small and interval included null. Key negative findings: H2, H6, H10-H12, H16, H43. BAF specificity remains strong: H44 accepted/closed, H43 rejected/closed. Remaining weak unresolved/deferred: H1, H3, H4, H7, H13, H14, H15, H59, H60. Next priorities: complete specificity H61/H62; driver-marker follow-ups H48, H41, H63, H40, H42; if resources allow, independent validation of H9 and/or H5 primary. Treat all conclusions as observational associations in supplied lung cell-line dataset, not causality or generalizability."
